WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:09.760
Guys, the Overwatch League may have only one season to its name but it’s already provided

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:12.440
us with some unforgettable caster calls.

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:13.200
From the hype.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:21.160
(Casting)

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:22.560
to hilarious wordplay.

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:26.880
(Casting)

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:29.480
To moments so intense, the casters

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:31.600
physically buckled under the pressure.

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:36.360
(Casting)

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:40.800
It’s safe to say some of these calls will go down as truly iconic in the history of

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:42.000
pro Overwatch.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:47.240
And if you wanna be as Iconic as I am now, well you can pick up this t-shirt and plenty more

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:49.920
at theScore esports new online merch department.

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:54.080
A truly iconic link, will be pinned in the comment section below.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:01:01.520
Now without further ado, let's get on with it with the top 10 caster calls from Overwatch League Season 1.

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:09.040
Kicking off our list at number 10 is the moment when Carpe effectively became the first player

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:13.480
in the Overwatch League to go absolutely HAM on Widowmaker.

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:18.680
Even casters Doa and Montecristo seemed as
if they were taken off-guard by Carpe’s

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:19.840
lethal skills.

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:52.160
(Casting)

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:58.600
Sometimes a play is so absurdly sick, that the only sentiment a caster can muster is rage.

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:03.380
Coming in at number nine is Uber becoming audibly upset with TviQ as the player takes

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:07.160
out an airborne Pharah with Genji’s Deflection ability.

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:19.880
(Casting)

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:24.320
Uber is revered not only for his incredible casting, but for the shamelessness of his

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:28.440
wordplay which is why this one comes in at
number eight on our list.

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:33.440
When confronted with the Houston Outlaws’ cheesy use of a shield-protected Bastion turret,

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:38.530
the Philadelphia Fusion made such an improbable
turn that Uber couldn’t help but dish out

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:41.720
a juicy pun at the Outlaws’ expense.

00:02:41.720 --> 00:03:23.040
(Casting)

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:28.840
During the London Spitfire’s Stage 1 Final against the New York Excelsior, Profit guided a game-winning

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:32.160
JunkRat ultimate into the back of Ark’s Mercy.

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:37.200
And coming in at number seven is Semmler’s
glorious effort to add Profit’s hundred-thousand-dollar

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:42.000
Rip-Tire to esports’ already-existing group of monetized ultimates.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:55.880
(Casting)

00:03:55.880 --> 00:03:58.910
The number sixth entry on our list reminds us that Overwatch

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:04.360
is a game in which the line between carry and support is especially fuzzy.

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:09.590
Watch and listen as Uber tries to keep Neptuno in check as the Fusion player’s Battle Mercy

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:15.760
leverages a two-man revival into a triple kill, redefining what it means to ‘swoop in for the rescue’.

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:43.780
(Casting)

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:48.920
A big part of being in the Overwatch League is being a god with projectile heroes.

00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:54.139
And coming in at number five, we have Hagopeun who puts on a Zenyatta snipeshow against the

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:55.680
Boston Uprising.

00:04:55.680 --> 00:05:00.620
Even a caster as composed and calculating as Uber couldn’t help but shout random phrases

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:03.320
as the spiritual support held his ground.

00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:43.320
(Casting)

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:44.200
Coming in at number

00:05:44.219 --> 00:05:50.139
four we have the call that followed the infamous 4K that Pine scored against the London Spitfire

00:05:50.139 --> 00:05:51.469
in Junkertown.

00:05:51.469 --> 00:05:56.779
Doa and Montecristo can’t even watch the replay of Pine’s final flick shot without

00:05:56.800 --> 00:06:00.000
entering a state of utter disbelief.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:39.600
(Casting)

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:45.000
The third spot on our list sees the return of Carpe’s already legendary Widowmaker.

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:50.200
Watch as the South Korean assassin squeezes off two of the nastiest headshots you’ll

00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:55.840
ever see in such quick succession that even the Rap God himself struggles to keep his composure.

00:06:56.000 --> 00:07:25.840
(Casting)

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:31.479
If there’s anything to learn from our runner-up, it’s that nicknames can only ever be earned.

00:07:31.479 --> 00:07:36.669
During New York Excelsior’s Stage 1 match against the Houston Outlaws, ‘Big Boss Pine’

00:07:36.669 --> 00:07:42.619
conducted a master class in McCree, causing Doa to bellow Pine’s now notorious nickname

00:07:42.619 --> 00:07:49.280
with such fervour that it solidified the size of the South Korean superstar’s authority once and for all.

00:07:49.360 --> 00:08:17.720
(Casting)

00:08:17.720 --> 00:08:23.800
Taking the top spot on our list is an epic yet innocuous line that won’t soon be forgotten.

00:08:23.800 --> 00:08:28.960
During the LA Gladiators’ first playoff match against Spitfire, Uber made an observation

00:08:28.979 --> 00:08:34.070
about Surefour’s exhilarating decision to ’chill in base’ -- which has since come

00:08:34.070 --> 00:08:39.680
to be regarded as one of the greatest strategic maneuvers in the game's competitive history.

00:08:39.920 --> 00:09:23.760
(Casting)

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:28.560
Well guys that's our list for this week, now did we miss out on your favourite Overwatch League

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:34.160
Season 1 caster call, well let us know in the comments below, leave a like and don't forget to sub to the channel.

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:35.360
See ya next week.

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:41.600
Thanks for watching. If you want more great content just like this, be sure to hit the subscribe button.

